   <p class="step" id="step-1">
                     <!--  "step-1"<br> -->
* wildcard numbers , no matter how large a number is it can only end with the digits 0 to 9.
<br><br>
Call these *wildcard , this is similar to the star (*) in regular expressions.
<br><br>
All integer numbers end with one of the values from the top row.
<br><br>
Looking for prime numbers can be limited to a subset of all numbers ; after arbitrarily sorting out numbers divisible by 2's , 3's , 5's.
<br><br>
There is a subset of numbers that end with 1 , 3 , 7 , 9 ; these numbers create unique formulas to assist in the search for prime numbers.
<br><br>
Defining these symbols as *1 , *3 ,*7 , *9 , and calling them *wildcard numbers , several sorts and sub-sorts
can be created and implemented to look for Prime Suspects (<u>ps</u>) (possible primes).
<br><br>
All the work that follows is a series of matrix math. Operating on several formulas and arrays simultaneously.
<br><br>
In the end it has become a study of integer values , and the subset of prime numbers.
<br><br>
The summation of all the work leads to two series ; a 'right' and 'left' where each series is +6 , +6 , +6 , +12. 
<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-2" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-2"<br> -->
These are the first eight prime numbers that make up *fam.
<br><br>
2 , 3 , 5 and are eliminated from the initial search.
<br><br>
These *fam numbers are a subset of the *wildcard numbers.
<br><br>
There are eight numbers that make the first group of primes. The bottom row.
<br><br>
A number is a ps (prime suspect) until verified.
<br><br>
These are a subset of *wildcard ; call them *famXX.
<br><br>
*fam01 any number that ends with *1 and satisfies the formula:
<br>
*fam01 = (ps - 01)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam07 = (ps - 07)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam11 = (ps - 11)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam13 = (ps - 13)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam17 = (ps - 17)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam19 = (ps - 19)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam23 = (ps - 23)/30 is an integer.
<br><br>
*fam29 = (ps - 29)/30 is an integer.

<br><br>


   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-3" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-3"<br> -->
This chart shows the difference between two planes. The numbers are separated by 10.
<br><br>
The one plane will have a name of R (right) for { 1 , 7 , 13 , 19 }.
<br><br>
The other plane will have a name of L (left) for { 11 , 17 , 23 , 29 }.
<br><br>
This is not an arbitrary name as will be show , later.
<br><br>
The ps (prime suspects) only end in four digits: { 1 , 3 , 7 , 9 }
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-4" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-4"<br><br> -->

There is a common value that internally separates the values inside both R and L: 6.
<br><br>
1 to 7 is 6 ; 7 to 13 is 6 ; 13 to 19 is 6.
<br><br>
11 to 17 is 6 ; 17 to 23 is 6 ; 23 to 29 is 6.
<br><br>
There are two values 10 and 6 that drive ps (prime suspects).
<br><br>
   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-5" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-5"<br><br> -->

Listing the values from 0 to 30 as a group ; the first group.
<br><br>
The name of a group will be given the symbol (g) [group]
<br><br>
This is designated as (g)0 in the upper left corner.
<br><br>
Inside the chart are the formulas for R & L.
<br><br>
This chart is balanced , it starts with a 0 and ends with a 0.
<br><br>
The midpoint is 15 ; there are 31 things , an odd number.
<br><br>
All the ps numbers for a given (g) are balanced by there location on the chart. They are equal distant from the ends and the midpoint.
<br><br>
01 + 29 =30
<br><br>
07 + 23=30
<br><br>
11 + 19=30
<br><br>
13 + 17=30
<br><br>
Since the next possible ps is 30 away from the prior for any *fam.
<br><br>
*fam01 is the series:
<br> { 1 , 31 , 61 , 91 , . . . }
<br><br>
*fam07 is the series:
<br> { 7 , 37 , 67 , 97 , . . . }
<br><br>
*fam11 is the series:
<br> { 11 , 41 , 71 , 101 , . . . }
<br><br>
This is the same for all *fam numbers.
<br><br>
Top row is the following formula:
<br>
(6 * ?) + 1	for ? = { 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 }
<br>
The difference in the signs R is: + 1
<br>
1	=	(6 * 0) + 1
<br>
7	=	(6 * 1) + 1
<br>
13	=	(6 * 2) + 1
<br>
19	=	(6 * 3) + 1
<br>
Name this as the 'right' or 'R'

<br><br>
Bottom row is the following formula:
<br>
(6 * ?) - 1	for ? = { 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 }
<br>
The difference in the signs L is: - 1
<br>
11	=	(6 * 2) - 1
<br>
17	=	(6 * 3) - 1	
<br>
23	=	(6 * 4) - 1
<br>
29	=	(6 * 5) - 1	
<br>
Name this as the 'left' or 'L'

<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-6" style="display: none;">
                    <!--   "step-6"<br><br> -->
This chart shows by arrows and color the pairs to make 30.
<br><br>
The mid point of the group (g) is 15 ; the symbol (g) means a group of thirty one numbers.
<br><br>
The (g) is bracketed by two numbers ending in zero ; and in the series: 
<br>
{ 0 , 30 , 60 , 90 , . . . }
<br><br>
The prime suspect numbers are balanced and make pairs equal distance from 15 for any (g).
<br><br>
The following is the distance from 15.
<br>15 +/- 14 = 1 or 29
<br>15 +/- 8 = 7 or 23
<br>15 +/- 4 = 11 or 19
<br>15 +/- 2 = 13 or 17
<br>Each pair adds up to 30	
<br><br>
The distance between each next pair is:					
<br>	6 = 14 - 8
<br>	4 = 8 - 4
<br>	2 = 4 - 2
<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-7" style="display: none;">
                  <!--     "step-7" <br><br> -->
The balance is also between R & L and between the two planes.
<br><br>
The midpoint of 15 is the center point from left to right and top to bottom.
<br><br>
Look at *fam11 (L) on the bottom plane and *fam19 (R) on the top plane ; they are balanced to the middle (15) and to the ends (0 & 30). 
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-8" style="display: none;">
                  <!--     "step-8"<br><br> -->
In the chart are the eight formulas to solve each of *fam.
<br><br>
As (g) increases from 0 to 1 the formulas will simultaneous solve all equations , this is matrix math.
<br><br>
(g)n:  *fam + (n)(3)(10) = ps
<br><br>
This can be rearranged as:
<br>
(g)n:  *fam + (n)(3)(2)(5)  = ps
<br>
or
<br>
(g)n:  *fam + (n)(1)(2)(3)(5)  = ps
<br>
the first four primes times some n plus { 1 , 7 , 13 , 19 } = R
<br><br>
(g)n:  *fam + (n)(1)(2)(3)(5)  = ps 
<br>
the first four primes times some n plus { 11, 17 , 23 , 29 } = L
<br><br><br>
Just looking at one *fam ; *fam07.
<br>
(g)0 : 7 + (0)(3)(10) = 7
<br>
(g)1 : 7 + (1)(3)(10) = 37
<br>
(g)2 : 7 + (2)(3)(10) = 97
<br>
(g)3 : 7 + (3)(3)(10) = 127
<br><br>

<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-9" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-9" <br><br> -->
The reverse calculation can be applied to any ps to find what (g) it belongs to.
<br><br>
Pick a number ending in 1 , 3 , 7 , 9.
<br><br>
Example: 143
<br><br>
There are two possible *fam associated , either *fam13 or *fam23.
<br><br>
Take 143 - 13 = 130 ; divide by 30 = 4.3333 - it is not an integer
<br><br>
Next 143 - 23 = 120 ; divide by 30 = 4 - it is an integer.
<br><br>
The answer is (g)4.
<br><br>
If neither equation solves to an integer the number is divisible by 3 and therefore not a ps.
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-10" style="display: none;">
                     <!--  "step-10" <br><br> -->
This chart uses *fam06 to find ps.
<br><br>
The top half is *fam06 + 1 - moving right on the number line.
<br><br>
The bottom half is *fam06 - 1 - moving left on the number line.
<br><br>
The R has values of { 0 ,1 , 2 , 3}.
<br><br>
The L has values of { 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 }

<br><br>

<br><br>
   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-11" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-11"<br><br> -->
Next is looking for ps using odd numbers. The (2n + 1) in each formula is an odd number.
<br><br>
The (3 * 5) portion is is the midpoint of the (g).
<br><br>
The last portion of the equations is the value added or subtracted from the midpoint.
<br><br>
The values { 14 , 8 , 2 , 4 } balance from R to L.
<br><br>
Next , see the + and - signs flip at the midpoint. Balanced again.
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-12" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-12"<br><br> -->
Using an odd number times 15 and a difference of 10 (-5 to +5) shows an additional set of equations.
<br><br>
The odd number times 15 is always the midpoint of a (g).
<br><br>
Since *1 (*wildcard1) is 6 away from *7 (*wildcard7) within a (g) ; the second element -9 and -3 are 6 away.
<br><br>
Since *3 (*wildcard3) is 6 away from *9 (*wildcard9) within a (g) ; the second element +3 and +9 are 6 away.
<br><br>
This is just a different view with different formulas to look for ps.
<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-13" style="display: none;">
                     <!--  "step-13"<br><br> -->
Placing the equations in *fam order , notice how the digits ( 3 , 9 ) and ( 5 ) ; and the signs ( + , - ) are balanced symmetrically.
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-14" style="display: none;">
                     <!--  "step-14"<br><br> -->
Moving into (g)1 here is a recap of the prior formulas.
<br><br>
Note that 49 is Xed  out as it is not prime (np).
<br><br>
<br><br>
   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-15" style="display: none;">
                  <!--     "step-15"<br><br> -->
Moving into (g)2 here is a recap of the prior formulas.
<br><br>
Note that 77 is Xed  out as it is not prime (np).
<br><br>
<br><br>


   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-16" style="display: none;">
                    <!--   "step-16"<br><br> -->
Moving into (g)3 here is a recap of the prior formulas.
<br><br>
Note that 91 and 119  are Xed out as they are not prime (np).
<br><br>
<br><br>


   </p>


   <p class="step" id="step-17" style="display: none;">
                    <!--   "step-17" -->
 This is a screen print of an attached HTML document. 
 <br> <br>
The four pages that follow show data from an XLSM file for (g)0 , (g)1 , (g)2 , (g)3.
At the bottom of each text column is a link to fire up this web page where you can enter your own (g)n value and calculate the entire (g). Size might be an issue and you might have to do resize on the web page. 
<br><br>
The key is this is where the labels R and L come from.
<br><br> 
The original difficult was the over lap between *fam11 and *fam19 and being able to present the sign + and - properly. Also , clarity in the data presentation.
<br><br> 
The solution was to split the equations into R and L ( meaning right or left of the equal sign ) ; the the two separate planes are separated by 10. Also , R +1 on the number line ; and L -1 on the number line compared to *fam06. 
<br><br>
The center column is *fam06 ; and +1 for R ; and -1 for L. For the sake of brevity , size , and easy of programming several columns are missing from the web page.   
<br><br>
If a number is a prime a "=P" flag is set ; if the number is not prime (np) a "=NP" flag is set and the flag flashes. 

<br><br>
<br><br>
<br><br>
  </p>

   <p class="step" id="step-18" style="display: none;">
                     <!--  "step-18"<br><br> -->
<u>See link at the bottom to calculate your own (g).</u>
<br><br>

All of the prior formulas lead up to this chart. This is where R and L are clearly shown.
<br><br>
First is the input field "(g) or PSL" as data is entered the matrix recalculates and displays new data.
<br><br>
The colored bars are matched pairs.
<br>
blue: &nbsp;&nbsp;*fam01 and *fam29.
<br>
red: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*fam07 and *fam23.
<br>
green: *fam11 and *fam19.
<br>
gold: &nbsp;&nbsp;*fam13  and *fam17.
<br><br>
The right is some *fam06 +1.
<br>
The left is some *fam06 -1.
<br>
Think in terms of a number line:
<br>R (right) is plus ; L (left) is minus.
<br><br>
Each ps , on the R or L is separated by 6.
<br><br>
The midpoint 15 is color code yellow.
<br><br>
The center column labeled as "*fam06 == cnt6" tells what value of *fam06 and the count (cnt6) to display the value.
<br><br>
The two columns to the left and right of that column are just a counter , a ruler to show the (g).
<br><br>
Outside of those columns is the actual calculation of all the values in the (g). The title boxes are labeled "left" and "right".
<br><br>
There are (g)0 , (g)1 , (g)2 and (g)3 over this and the next three screens.
<br><br>
There is a flag field on the outer edge that prints "Prime" or "Not Prime" (in a lighter color font).

