   <p class="step" id="step-30" style="display: none;">
                    <!--   "step-30" -->
This is where it all began , I was looking for prime numbers ; but I wanted to teach myself EXCEL. I figured it was a good model to train on and look for numbers.
<br><br>
I knew all kinds of commands from the BASIC programming language. Did they apply to EXCEL ?
<br><br>
Yes , and more  . . .
<br><br>
Next , there were added features like mod() function and conditional formatting. The cells in yellow are conditional colored if mod() = 0 from the top row and the second column.
<br><br>
It was a quick visual to help look for primes. The second column is also color coded when the number is a ps (prime suspect)  
<br><br>
Most of the work done here was done with EXCEL ; quick , easy , fast. 
<br><br>
<br><br>

   </p>
   <p class="step" id="step-31" style="display: none;">
                    <!--   "step-31" -->
This chart is similar to the prior , except all the even numbers are filtered out.
<br><br>
The gray colored cells are the integer value of the square root of the row times the column.
<br><br>
Another quick marker to limit how far to look for a non zero mod(). Meaning factors and therefor not prime. 
<br><br>
   </p>
   <p class="step" id="step-32" style="display: none;">
                      <!-- "step-32" -->
